                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A dainty JRPG that desires to be a blast to the past. Shadows of Adam is a manufactured revival of the classic turn based combat games of the NES and SNES era, featuring a polished gameplay, cleaned from the most annoying aspects of previous generation entries such as: grinding, slow combat, random encounters and restricted mana (here, named AP, gets regenerated). \nHere there is a precise number of enemies per area, blocking treasures and ways to progress a dungeon, so you can explore freely an area for secrets without the fear of getting interrupted.\n\nStory and characters imitate that classic JRPG narration, (you can expect elemental crystals here too) albeit this time, it doesn’t shy away from being playfully unserious.\nAs well the cast of protagonists is distinct and they all have their personality and motives for adventure, even if there aren’t many bartering scenes, their chemistry is not boring at all.\n\nIt’s not even lengthy, requiring you less than 10 hours to reach the endgame, and maybe 3 more hours for the optional content. It’s a small adventure that doesn’t want to be complicated in the gear or combat system but just brims with blunt fun. Although it may not be enjoyable if you are searching for something complicated or difficult; that’s why I highly recommend to purchase the DLC as well, which is bountiful of challenges, new mechanics, and superbosses that will put to test your mastery on the game.\n\nAnd the pixel art is too is colorful and detailed enough to be pleasing to the eye.\n\nA must for anyone nostalgic or not, who craves to replay JRPG of decades ago, but with a new breath of fresh air.",
